course: everyday
lesson: 127
title: Excuse me, and good manners at the table
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: "Excuse me" is a small key that opens many doors: you say it to get past people, to get a shop assistant's attention, after a burp by accident, and "May I please be excused?" before you leave the table.
explain: Eating together is shared, so you wait until everyone is seated and served before you start, unless the host says "Start".
explain: You ask "Could you please pass the salt?" instead of reaching across, you take a fair share from a shared dish with the serving spoon, and you offer the last piece to others before taking it.
explain: When you have finished, you put your knife and fork together on the plate, and you wipe your mouth with a napkin, not your sleeve.
explain: A girl in Boston keeps her tablet in her room at dinner time; instead she tells her family about her day, which is what meals are for.
fact: You say "Excuse me" and wait for people to move when you need to get past them.
fact: "Excuse me" is a polite way to get a stranger's attention, like a shop assistant's.
fact: After a burp or passing wind by accident, you say "Excuse me" quietly.
fact: Before leaving the table early, you ask "May I please be excused?"
fact: When someone sneezes, many people in English-speaking countries say "Bless you".
fact: You wait until everyone is seated and served before eating, unless the host says to start.
fact: You ask for things to be passed instead of reaching across the table.
fact: Knife and fork placed together on the plate show you have finished.
fact: Trying a small bite of a new food, if it is safe for you, is polite.
fact: Elbows stay off the table while you eat.
fact: Soup is eaten quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon, without slurping.
fact: Devices stay away from the table, because meals are a time to talk.
fact: From a shared dish you use the serving spoon and take a fair share, and you offer the last piece before taking it.
exercise: You need to get past people in a narrow aisle. What do you do? | Say "Excuse me" and wait for them to move | Push past quickly without a word | Squeeze under their arms | Wait silently behind them until somebody finally notices you
exercise: You want to ask a shop assistant where the pens are. How do you start? | "Excuse me, ..." | "Oi, you!" | "Tell me where the pens are" | Tap them hard on the back
exercise: You burp by accident at the table. What do you say? | "Excuse me", quietly | Nothing, and pretend it was your brother | "That was a good one!" loudly | "Bless you"
exercise: You need to leave the table before the others finish. What do you say? | "May I please be excused?" | Nothing, you just get up | "I'm going now, bye" | "Hurry up and finish"
exercise: Someone sneezes next to you. What is a common friendly thing to say? | "Bless you" | "Excuse me" | "Thank you" | "Sorry"
exercise: Dinner is ready but your dad is still bringing the plates. What do you do? | Wait until everyone is seated and served | Start eating so your food stays warm | Eat from the serving dish while you wait so you don't get too hungry | Take your plate to another room
exercise: You want the salt at the other end of the table. What is polite? | Ask "Could you please pass the salt?" | Stand up and reach across everyone | Climb onto your chair to grab it | Eat without salt and complain about it
exercise: You have finished eating. How can you show it? | Put your knife and fork together on the plate | Push your plate into the middle of the table | Leave your fork stuck in the food | Stand up and walk away straight away without a word to anyone
exercise: You are offered a food you have never tried, and it is safe for you. What is polite? | Try a small bite | Say "Yuck" and push it away | Hide it under your napkin | Ask for something else straight away
exercise: Where should your elbows be while you are eating? | Off the table | On the table, holding your head up | On your neighbour's chair | Under your plate
exercise: You need to wipe your mouth. What do you use? | A napkin | Your sleeve | The tablecloth | The back of your hand
exercise: You want to watch videos on your tablet at dinner. What is the rule? | Keep devices away from the table | Watch with the sound off | Watch only during the pudding | Show your family the videos instead
exercise: You take potatoes from a shared dish. What is right? | Use the serving spoon and take a fair share | Use your own fork and take as many as you like | Take them with your fingers | Take the whole dish to your place
exercise: One last piece of pizza is left. What do you do? | Ask if anyone else would like it first | Grab it before anyone notices | Cut it into tiny pieces for yourself so it looks like less | Lick it so nobody else wants it
exercise: How do you eat soup politely? | Quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon | Slurping loudly to show the cook that it is very tasty | Drinking it straight from the bowl | Blowing bubbles in it to cool it
test: A boy in Glasgow pushes past a lady on the bus without a word and she almost falls. What should he have done? | Said "Excuse me" and waited for her to move | Pushed harder so it was quicker | Waited for the next bus | Climbed over the seats
test: Why is reaching across the table for the ketchup a bad idea? | It is rude and can knock things over | Because ketchup is only for adults | Because your arm might get tired | Because it makes the ketchup go cold
test: At a family dinner in Canada, everyone has food except Grandpa. What do you do? | Wait until Grandpa is served too | Start, because your food is ready | Eat half and then wait | Ask Grandpa to hurry up
test: Why do many families keep phones and tablets away from dinner? | Meals are a time to talk with each other | Because food makes screens break | Because games are only allowed at breakfast | Because phones cannot work in the kitchen
test: Your friend's mum serves a curry you have never eaten. It is safe for you. What is polite? | Try a small bite | Refuse it without trying | Say it smells strange | Ask for chips instead
test: A girl in Sydney wipes her mouth on her sleeve at a restaurant. What would be better? | Using a napkin | Using the tablecloth | Licking her lips clean | Waiting until she gets home
test: Why do we take a fair share from a dish everyone eats from? | So that everyone has enough | Because the cook counts each piece | Because big portions are unhealthy for everyone | Because the dish will break if it is too empty
test: You want to go and play while the adults are still eating. What do you say first? | "May I please be excused?" | "I've finished, so bye" | Nothing, they will not notice | "You eat too slowly"
test: oops burped at dinner lol | Say excuse me quietly | Burp again to make them laugh | Pretend it was your brother | Say nothing and keep eating
test: want the ketchup, its way over there | Ask someone to pass it | Stand up and reach across the table | Climb onto your chair to get it | Wait until everyone has left
test: done eatin, can i just go play | Ask, may I please be excused | Just leave the table quietly | Shout bye and run | Take your plate to your room
test: one roastie left, im having it | Offer it to others before you take it | Grab it quick before anyone sees | Take it with your fingers | Cut it in two and eat both halves
explain: A boy in Ohio talked with his mouth full and laughed at the same time; a piece of food went down the wrong way, and he choked until an adult helped him.
explain: A girl in Leeds pushed past people in a crowded corridor without saying excuse me; a younger child was knocked over and hurt his wrist.
fact: Talking or laughing with food in your mouth raises the chance of choking.
fact: If someone is choking and cannot talk or cough, get an adult at once, who can give firm back blows.
fact: Someone's «ex» is a boyfriend, girlfriend or partner they used to be with, and «not for little ears» means grown-up talk that is not for children.
fact: Pushing past people in crowded places can knock someone over, especially small children.
test: mouth full of crisps, telling a joke rn | Swallow first, you can choke if you laugh with food in | Talk fast, it's fine as long as you don't laugh too hard | Laugh while chewing | Stuff in more first
test: corridor's packed, gonna shove thru | Say excuse me and wait, shoving can knock someone over | Shove, everyone does | Run through the gaps | Push the small kids aside, they're light and won't really get hurt
test: mate's choking and can't talk or cough | Get an adult now, they do back blows | Give him a drink | Make him lie down | Wait to see if it passes
test: did u hear Leo laughed with a mouthful of sausage at lunch n went proper blue, they say the ambulance came n everything | Don't laugh or talk with food in; the ambulance part may be exaggerated | Laughing while eating is totally safe as long as you are sitting down properly | Only sausages make people choke | Ambulances come every time someone coughs
test: apparently Priya burps at dinner and doesn't even say excuse me, she's disgusting, tell everyone | Don't spread it; burps happen, and excuse me quietly is the polite way | Tell everyone, she deserves it | Burping at dinner is a crime in some countries, so she must be punished | Excuse me is only said at school
test: my cousin says some big kid shoved thru the corridor and a little kid broke both arms, I swear | Say excuse me and wait; shoving can hurt, though both arms may be blown up | Shoving is fine if you're in a hurry | Little kids never get hurt in corridors | It's all true because your cousin said it, and cousins never make things up
exam: Identify the appropriate request when one wishes to leave the table before others have finished. | "May I please be excused?" | "I'm finished, see you later." | "Can everyone hurry up, please?" | "Excuse me" said after one has gone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises correct conduct in respect of a condiment, such as salt or ketchup, beyond one's reach? | One asks for it to be passed instead of reaching across | One stands up and reaches across the table for it | One waits until everyone has left the table | One takes it from a neighbour's plate quietly
exam: Identify the principal reason why talking or laughing with food in the mouth is discouraged. | It raises the chance of choking | It makes the food lose its taste | It causes the food to grow cold | It makes the meal last much longer
exam: Whereas a shared dish has been placed upon the table, select the response consistent with established manners. | Use the serving spoon, take a fair share, offer the last piece | Use one's own fork and take as much as one wishes | Wait until the host has eaten the whole dish first | Take the last piece first before others can claim it
test: at Christmas dinner uncle asked my big sis loudly "so, got a boyfriend yet?" n she went red | He asked if she has someone special; she does not have to answer, so do not join in teasing her | Ask her louder too | Tell everyone her secrets | Laugh until she cries
test: at the table nan said "excuse me, that's not for little ears" when auntie talked bout her ex | Her ex is a partner she used to be with; it is grown-up private talk, so politely let them talk | Ask auntie for all the details | Tell her to stop talking | Shout "ex what?" loudly
test: my brother went "Ella's got a boyfriend" with his mouth full at tea | He means Ella has someone special she likes; talking with your mouth full is rude and so is teasing her, so stop | It means Ella is eating | It means Ella is in trouble | Ask her loudly who it is
